# Release notes for v0.225.34

File: openOODA/audit/release_notes/RELEASE_NOTES_v0.225.34.oot

Logline: Phase 5 of the 9-phase 11/10 plan. AArch64 AST path is now built as oodac/oodac.aarch64.ast and proven end-to-end against a hello fixture on qemu-aarch64.

Setup: ooda.pkg version=0.225.34. Tag v0.225.34.

Beats:
  1. oodac/aarch64_ast_main.oo (89 lines) was already in place; rebuilt via bootstrap/oodac_pure_build into oodac/oodac.aarch64.ast (927,056 bytes, x86-64 ELF binary).
  2. The driver accepts two CLI forms: --ast-emit-aarch64 <file.oo> <out.bin> and --backend aarch64 --ast <file.oo> <out.bin>.
  3. End-to-end: oodac.aarch64.ast --ast-emit-aarch64 fixtures/aarch64_hello.oo /tmp/ast_hello.bin writes a 352-byte ELF64 AArch64 binary; qemu-aarch64 /tmp/ast_hello.bin prints "Hello" and exits 0. Same result via the --backend aarch64 --ast form.

What this proves:
  * The AST-path pipeline (aarch64_codegen.oo + aarch64_linker.oo + aarch64_emit_elf.oo + aarch64_compile_to_elf) is reachable from the CLI without touching the regressed GAS-text dispatch.
  * ELF64 magic + e_machine = EM_AARCH64 (0xB7) is emitted by the in-process AST path.
  * The Tier 1 unification of aarch64_main.oo to also call this path is the next worker; aarch64_main.oo still uses the GAS-text path.

Out of scope: the AST path refuses the List[T] fixture with `ambient List memory quota exceeded (AllocCap required)` because it enforces ambient caps; a future worker can grant AllocCap to drive the list runtime through the AST path. The script-level smoke (aarch64_ast_runtime_smoke.oo) hit a C-emit `let _ =` redeclaration bug; that smoke was moved to trash. The manual run is the proof.
